{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","count":12,"questions":[{"id":51595,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Consider a regular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>-</mo></math>gon with its vertices on the unit circle. With one vertex fixed, draw straight lines to the other <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math> vertices. Call them <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></math> and denote their lengths by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></math> respectively. Then the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></math> is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>50</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F554a0e69-d15b-4cf6-a9a9-37179add03f9-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F554a0e69-d15b-4cf6-a9a9-37179add03f9-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F554a0e69-d15b-4cf6-a9a9-37179add03f9-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 203px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Now, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>…</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>…</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math><br />\nso <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>…</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>|</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>…</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>)</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>…</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7504847d75218aeb76d"},{"id":51596,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let $z_{1}, z_{2}, \\ldots \\ldots, z_{7}$ be the vertices of a regular heptagon that is inscribed in the unit circle with centre at the origin in the complex plane. Let $\\mathrm{w}=\\sum_{1 \\leq \\mathrm{i} \\lt  \\mathrm{j} \\leq 7} \\mathrm{z}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{z}_{\\mathrm{j}}$, then $|\\mathrm{w}|$ is equal to","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":0,"solution":"given expression can be written as\n\n\\frac{\\left(z_{1}+z_{2}+\\ldots+z_{7}\\right)^{2}-\\sum_{i=1}^{7} z_{i}^{2}}{2}=\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq-7} z_{i} z_{j}\n\nSum of all seven root of unity $=0$ (By property) $z_{1}^{2}+z_{2}^{2}+z_{3}^{2}+\\ldots .+z_{7}^{2}=0$ (By property)","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2135a5b49b468837677"},{"id":51597,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In the complex plane, let $z_{1}=\\sqrt{3}+i$ and $\\mathrm{z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}-\\mathrm{i}$ be two adjacent vertices of an n-sided regular polygon centered at origin. Then $\\mathrm{n}$ equals","options":["4","6","8","12"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FKBErBTFTjhu_jJ526oJU5Wb2_VZWWO8uS5_rfBgVX58.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FKBErBTFTjhu_jJ526oJU5Wb2_VZWWO8uS5_rfBgVX58.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FKBErBTFTjhu_jJ526oJU5Wb2_VZWWO8uS5_rfBgVX58.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\frac{2 \\pi}{n}=\\frac{\\pi}{3}n=6$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d7b46529ee3bc126"},{"id":51598,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"On any given arc of positive length on the unit circle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> in the complex plane.","options":["there need not be any root of unity","there lies exactly one root of unity","there are more than one but finitely many roots of unity","there are infinitely many roots of unity"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>We have,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>All roots of unity lie on arc of circle.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> There are infinitely many roots of unity.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6a0edef5532f5b41ac0"},{"id":51599,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>y</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></math> be complex numbers on the unit circle such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Then the number of ordered pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>","infinite"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>It is given that complex numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>y</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></math> are on the unit circle such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>\n<p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mfenced><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mfenced><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mfenced><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mover><mi>w</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>z</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>w</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n<p> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∵</mo></math> The number of solution of equations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> is eight </p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F42c29170-7b42-49d6-a8e3-33fb01bc23f6-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F42c29170-7b42-49d6-a8e3-33fb01bc23f6-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F42c29170-7b42-49d6-a8e3-33fb01bc23f6-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Number of ordered pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is also eight.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7082867e2ccbf93f325"},{"id":51600,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. We say that a real number is algebraic if it is a root of a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then,","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> is algebraic but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> is not algebraic","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> is algebraic but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> is not algebraic","both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> are algebraic","neither <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> nor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> is algerbraic"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>We have,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><msup><mrow/><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><msup><mrow/><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><msup><mrow/><mi>n</mi></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>60</mn></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>+</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn><none/><mprescripts/><none/><mn>60</mn></mmultiscripts><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>58</mn></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>Change all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sin</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> using the identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> equation with root <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math> so it is algebraic.</p>\n\n<p>Similarly, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6a2df43eba031b5e4a9"},{"id":51601,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let a be a fixed non-zero complex number with $|\\mathrm{a}| \\lt  1$ and $\\mathrm{w}=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{z}-\\mathrm{a}}{1-\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{z}}\\right)$. Where $\\mathrm{z}$ is a complex number. Then","options":["there exists a complex number $\\mathrm{z}$ with $|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1$ such that $|\\mathrm{w}|\\gt 1$","$|\\mathrm{w}|\\gt 1$ for all $\\mathrm{z}$ such that $|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1$","$|\\mathrm{w}| \\lt  1$ for all $\\mathrm{z}$ such that $|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1$","there exists $\\mathrm{z}$ such with $|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1$ and $|\\mathrm{w}|=1$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$|\\mathrm{a}| \\lt  1 \\& \\mathrm{w}=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{z}-\\mathrm{a}}{1-\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{z}}\\right) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{w}-\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{zw}=\\mathrm{z}-\\mathrm{a}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a}=\\mathrm{z}(1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w})\\mathrm{z}=\\frac{\\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a}}{1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w}}$\nGiven $|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1\\left|\\frac{\\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a}}{1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w}}\\right| \\lt  1 \\Rightarrow|\\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a}|^{2} \\lt  |1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w}|^{2}\\Rightarrow(\\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a})(\\overline{\\mathrm{w}}+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}}) \\lt  (1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w})(1+\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}})\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{w} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}+\\mathrm{w} \\overline{\\mathrm{a}}+\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}+\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\lt  1+\\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w}+\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}+\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{w} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{a}} \\cdot \\mathrm{w} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}-\\mathrm{w} \\overline{\\mathrm{w}}-\\mathrm{a} \\overline{\\mathrm{a}}+1\\gt 0\\Rightarrow|\\mathrm{a}|^{2}|\\mathrm{w}|^{2}-|\\mathrm{w}|^{2}|\\mathrm{a}|^{2}+1\\gt 0\\Rightarrow\\left(|\\mathrm{a}|^{2}-1\\right)\\left(|\\mathrm{w}|^{2}-1\\right)\\gt 0$\nGiven $|\\mathrm{a}| \\lt  1 \\quad|\\mathrm{w}|^{2}-1 \\lt  0|\\mathrm{w}| \\lt  1 \\&|\\mathrm{z}| \\lt  1$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21364f8dba511a1b342"},{"id":51602,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If $\\mathrm{z}$ is a complex number satisfying $\\left|z^{3}+z^{-3}\\right| \\leq 2$, then the maximum possible value of $\\left|z+z^{-1}\\right|$ is","options":["2","$\\sqrt[3]{2}$","$2 \\sqrt{2}$","1"],"answer":0,"solution":"\n\\left|z^{3}+\\frac{1}{z^{3}}\\right| \\leq 2\n\nBut we know that $\\left|z^{3}+\\frac{1}{z^{3}}\\right| \\leq|z|^{3}+\\frac{1}{|z|^{3}}$\nSo when $|\\mathrm{z}|=1$ by $\\mathrm{AM} \\geq \\mathrm{GM}$\n|\\mathrm{z}|^{3}+\\frac{1}{|\\mathrm{z}|^{3}}=2\n\nNow $\\left|\\mathrm{z}+\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{z}}\\right| \\leq|\\mathrm{z}|+\\frac{1}{|\\mathrm{z}|}=2$\nMaximum value $=2$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213eda893dd993e0763"},{"id":51603,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let $I, \\omega$ and $\\omega^{2}$ be the cube roots of unity. The least possible degree of a polynomial, with real coefficients, having $2 \\mathrm{~m}^{2}, 3+4 \\mathrm{c}, 3+4 \\mathrm{c}^{2}$ and $5-\\omega-\\omega^{2}$ as roots is-","options":["4","5","6","8"],"answer":1,"solution":"roots $\\rightarrow 2 \\omega^{2}, 3+4 \\omega, \\quad 3+4 \\omega^{2}, \\quad 5-\\omega-\\omega^{2}$\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\alpha \\quad \\beta \\quad \\gamma \\quad \\delta \\\\\n\\delta=5-\\left(\\omega+\\omega^{2}\\right)=5-(-1)=6\n\\end{array}\n\nIf $\\alpha=2 \\omega^{2}$ is a root then $2 \\omega$ has to be a root too.\ntotal $\\rightarrow \\min .5$ roots, hence min. degree $\\rightarrow 5$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213afc61ddb106d55ae"},{"id":51604,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let $\\omega$ be a cube root of unity not equal to 1 . Then the maximum possible value of $\\left|a+b w+c w^{2}\\right|$ where $a$, b, $c \\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ is","options":["0","2","$\\sqrt{3}$","$1+\\sqrt{3}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\left|a+b w+c w^{2}\\right|\\quad|a-c+(b-c) w| \\quad$, for maximum value taking $a=1, c=-1, b=1\\left|a+b w+c w^{2}\\right|=|2+2 w|=2\\left|w^{2}\\right|=2$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213c54f1597f18d01ad"},{"id":51605,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The figure in the complex plane given by<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>","options":["a straight line","a circle","a parabola","an ellipse"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>z</mi><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\nLet<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>y</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> straight line</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7520a2b87700892058a","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login"},{"id":51606,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Complex Number","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math> is any root of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>21</mn><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt><mo>.</mo></math> Then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math> satisfies","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo><</mo><mi>S</mi><mo><</mo><mn>7</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi><mo><</mo><mn>13</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>We have,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>11</mn></mfrac><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>11</mn></mfrac><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>Root of equation are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></munderover><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>11</mn></mfrac><mi>i</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∵</mo><mn>1</mn><mo><</mo><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfenced><mo><</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo><</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo><</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>3</mn><mo><</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo><</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mn>3</mn><mo><</mo><mi>S</mi><mo><</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6c1b16753f441a9fd72"}]}